1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:23,260
This is a secret film containing restricted data.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Gentlemen, the purpose of this meeting

3
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:01,299
is to bring you up to date on the ANP program.

4
00:01:01,979 --> 00:01:05,099
During the three years since the last filmed report was made,

5
00:01:05,519 --> 00:01:07,840
a good many changes have taken place in the program.

6
00:01:08,560 --> 00:01:13,599
This briefing will cover that three-year period, 1956 through 1958.

7
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:19,460
For briefing purposes, we have broken this program into two sections.

8
00:01:20,060 --> 00:01:30,019
The manned program, which will be covered in this first film, and the unmanned, or Pluto, rover, and snap programs, which will be covered in a separate film.

9
00:01:30,939 --> 00:01:36,219
This, then, will be the Aircraft Nuclear Propulsion Status Report for 1958.

10
00:01:37,079 --> 00:01:41,560
The Aircraft Nuclear Propulsion Program was established in 1950

11
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:46,219
as a result of feasibility studies conducted under the NEPA and Lexington projects.

12
00:01:47,579 --> 00:01:51,039
General Kern was given command of the project in the Atomic Energy Commission,

13
00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:57,140
which was responsible for reactor development, and in the Air Force's Air Research and Development Command,

14
00:01:57,540 --> 00:02:00,620
which was responsible for engine and airframe development.

15
00:02:02,440 --> 00:02:05,760
During the next few years, the main objective of the program

16
00:02:05,760 --> 00:02:11,980
was the development of design information which would permit evaluation on a firm basis of flight feasibility

17
00:02:11,980 --> 00:02:14,780
and of the effort required to achieve such flight.

18
00:02:14,780 --> 00:02:26,039
In 1955, the Air Force established a requirement for a strategic bomber of extremely long range

19
00:02:26,039 --> 00:02:31,300
and capable of a supersonic dash to the target at high altitude.

20
00:02:31,300 --> 00:02:35,900
As a result of this requirement, the Aircraft Nuclear Propulsion Program was oriented toward

21
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:41,479
the development of such an aircraft, Weapon System 125A.

22
00:02:41,479 --> 00:02:46,659
was immediately started and continued steadily until major material problems resulted in

23
00:02:46,659 --> 00:02:51,919
reduced thrust to weight ratios, indicating an adequate supersonic dash radius was not

24
00:02:51,919 --> 00:02:59,120
feasible. This performance loss resulted in a reappraisal of the entire Weapon System

25
00:02:59,120 --> 00:03:06,819
125A program in the fall of 1956. Subsequently, a new program was begun with major emphasis

26
00:03:06,819 --> 00:03:11,019
placed solely on the development of a nuclear propulsion system.

27
00:03:11,019 --> 00:03:15,259
This program was placed under a dual agency aircraft nuclear

28
00:03:15,259 --> 00:03:19,659
propulsion office, with General Kern in charge.

29
00:03:19,659 --> 00:03:22,759
In March of 1958, a presidential directive

30
00:03:22,759 --> 00:03:28,019
approved a program development objective of a militarily useful aircraft.

31
00:03:28,019 --> 00:03:31,359
A short time later, in order to maintain airborne alert

32
00:03:31,359 --> 00:03:35,000
and high payload capabilities, penetration requirements

33
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:38,060
were reduced from high altitude to low altitude,

34
00:03:38,060 --> 00:03:41,879
and from supersonic to high subsonic.

35
00:03:41,879 --> 00:03:45,479
Low altitude also offers greater probability of survival

36
00:03:45,479 --> 00:03:47,539
during penetration.

37
00:03:47,539 --> 00:03:52,039
This new approach was known as the camel concept.

38
00:03:52,039 --> 00:03:55,439
Under this new camel concept, the nuclear-powered aircraft

39
00:03:55,439 --> 00:04:00,740
would patrol at high altitude for long periods of time.

40
00:04:00,740 --> 00:04:06,460
If necessary, the patrolling nuclear aircraft could proceed directly to predesignated missile release

41
00:04:06,460 --> 00:04:09,960
points, launch their long range missiles,

42
00:04:09,960 --> 00:04:13,500
and then drop to a low level to perform low altitude

43
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:17,240
evasive pattern penetration at near sonic speeds

44
00:04:17,240 --> 00:04:21,259
to either perform reconnaissance or deliver a lay down weapon

45
00:04:21,259 --> 00:04:24,459
against hard or hidden targets.

46
00:04:24,459 --> 00:04:27,680
Studies indicated that high subsonic penetration speeds

47
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:32,240
at low altitude would provide maximum mission protection.

48
00:04:32,240 --> 00:04:38,079
The difficulties of low altitude radar detection, as well as recent high altitude nuclear bomb tests

49
00:04:38,079 --> 00:04:41,120
in the Pacific, have indicated the advisability

50
00:04:41,120 --> 00:04:45,240
of low altitude penetration.

51
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:49,560
These missions could be performed without benefit of foreign bases and at reduced

52
00:04:49,560 --> 00:04:52,040
global operating costs.

53
00:04:52,040 --> 00:04:55,400
Besides the major propulsion program for manned aircraft,

54
00:04:55,400 --> 00:05:02,180
other associated areas under investigation include airframe, shielding, radiation effects,

55
00:05:02,180 --> 00:05:04,939
life sciences, and safety.

56
00:05:04,939 --> 00:05:08,639
In 1957, the ANP program was also

57
00:05:08,639 --> 00:05:12,319
expanded to take in the three additional nuclear projects

58
00:05:12,319 --> 00:05:17,960
designated Pluto, Rover, and Snap mentioned before.

59
00:05:17,960 --> 00:05:23,939
First, let's look at the manned aircraft program and the two different propulsion methods.

60
00:05:23,939 --> 00:05:28,579
They are General Electric's direct or air-cooled system

61
00:05:28,579 --> 00:05:32,379
and Pratt & Whitney's indirect or liquid metal cooled system.

62
00:05:33,560 --> 00:05:37,519
Of the two, the direct cycle system promised early flight capability

63
00:05:37,519 --> 00:05:40,599
and was selected for the major propulsion effort.

64
00:05:41,560 --> 00:05:46,899
In the GE system, a solid fuel element, air-cooled nuclear reactor,

65
00:05:47,199 --> 00:05:49,159
was located between twin engines.

66
00:05:50,099 --> 00:05:53,299
Air coming into the engine is compressed by the compressor blades,

67
00:05:54,039 --> 00:06:02,959
flows through the hot reactor where it is heated, and conversely cools the reactor and then passes through the turbines and the engine exhaust nozzle

68
00:06:02,959 --> 00:06:10,159
to produce thrust. This design makes it possible to modify the reactor core without disturbing the

69
00:06:10,159 --> 00:06:16,120
remainder of the system. Heat transfer reactor experiment number one utilizing this principle

70
00:06:16,120 --> 00:06:21,100
was conducted in January of 1956 at the National Reactor Test Station in Idaho.

71
00:06:21,100 --> 00:06:31,100
Inside the initial engine test facility, the reactor was brought to criticality and the engine switched from chemical burning heat to run entirely on reactor heat.

72
00:06:31,100 --> 00:06:38,100
The operation was successful, with temperatures of 1,400 degrees achieved at the turbine inlet.

73
00:06:38,100 --> 00:06:45,100
In the fall of 1957, the same reactor engine setup was used for a series of Heater 2 tests.

74
00:06:45,100 --> 00:06:50,140
the only major change was in the active reactor core which was modified by

75
00:06:50,140 --> 00:06:56,060
removing the seven center fuel tubes to form an octagonal hole by this means

76
00:06:56,060 --> 00:07:00,760
various solid moderators and fuel element materials could be inserted

77
00:07:00,760 --> 00:07:08,320
within the core and tested altogether three inserts were tested the first used

78
00:07:08,320 --> 00:07:14,120
a clad hydrated zirconium moderator the second an all ceramic insert made up of

79
00:07:14,120 --> 00:07:21,079
layers of beryllium oxide tubes with a beryllium oxide moderator. Incidentally during this test a

80
00:07:21,079 --> 00:07:27,600
record interior temperature of more than 2700 degrees Fahrenheit was reached, and the third

81
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:34,579
insert successfully tested an unclad hydrated zirconium moderator. A fourth test was then

82
00:07:34,579 --> 00:07:41,060
conducted to obtain experimental data on fission product releases. The airflow to one element was

83
00:07:41,060 --> 00:07:46,459
deliberately restricted to the point where the fuel element melted. Data from

84
00:07:46,459 --> 00:07:51,139
this and similar experiments are used in determining operational criteria so that

85
00:07:51,139 --> 00:07:56,120
nuclear operations will not result in a hazard to the public. A special

86
00:07:56,120 --> 00:08:00,079
high-power reactor system was then constructed and designated HTRE number

87
00:08:00,079 --> 00:08:04,040
three. It incorporated flight type reactor controls and a flight type

88
00:08:04,040 --> 00:08:09,620
reactor shield. This model illustrates HTRE number three, the first flight type

89
00:08:09,620 --> 00:08:15,540
horizontal 33 megawatt solid moderated reactor. This was a significant stride

90
00:08:15,540 --> 00:08:19,500
forward in propulsion development. The power plant simulated flight type

91
00:08:19,500 --> 00:08:24,920
geometry and structure. It was expected to provide valuable shielding data as

92
00:08:24,920 --> 00:08:31,180
well as solid moderator performance data. The initial engine test facilities were

93
00:08:31,180 --> 00:08:37,899
also modified for the HTRE number three tests. For example, a 400 foot tunnel was

94
00:08:37,899 --> 00:08:42,659
constructed to provide a shielded roadway beyond any possible contaminated area.

95
00:08:43,679 --> 00:08:49,019
This permitted changing shifts without shutting down the reactor, and in the event of reactor

96
00:08:49,019 --> 00:08:56,919
failure, the entire structure could be evacuated in seconds. Late in 1958, HTRE number three was

97
00:08:56,919 --> 00:09:03,159
moved to the initial engine test facility for its first test run. During a low-power test experiment

98
00:09:03,159 --> 00:09:06,199
to determine heating rates in the various components of the system,

99
00:09:06,199 --> 00:09:11,659
an error in electrical circuitry caused the reactor to exceed the design power level.

100
00:09:12,539 --> 00:09:16,699
Consequently, damage to the fuel cartridges and some moderator blocks occurred.

101
00:09:18,899 --> 00:09:23,679
Valuable data, however, were obtained during the operation and from the incident itself.

102
00:09:24,579 --> 00:09:28,159
As an example, the inherent safety of the reactor was demonstrated

103
00:09:28,159 --> 00:09:32,439
when the change in geometry resulted in an automatic shutdown of the reactor.

104
00:09:33,159 --> 00:09:39,159
These HTR number three experiments were expected to proceed in 1959.

105
00:09:39,159 --> 00:09:45,159
A short distance from the IET, a flight engine test facility was under construction.

106
00:09:45,159 --> 00:09:48,159
Here, full scale propulsion tests will be conducted,

107
00:09:48,159 --> 00:09:53,159
possibly with a nuclear propulsion system mounted on an air frame.

108
00:09:53,159 --> 00:09:57,159
This facility was to be ready for use in 1959.

109
00:09:57,159 --> 00:10:03,159
Other GE facilities at the Idaho test site include the shield test pool facilities,

110
00:10:03,159 --> 00:10:08,159
where the swimming pool reactor was to be ready for operation in 1959,

111
00:10:08,159 --> 00:10:11,159
and the newly completed low-power test facility,

112
00:10:11,159 --> 00:10:16,159
which provides for critical experiments on simulated reactor core configurations

113
00:10:16,159 --> 00:10:19,159
and fuel loading of actual cores.

114
00:10:19,159 --> 00:10:23,159
While these operations were going on in Idaho,

115
00:10:23,159 --> 00:10:32,279
GE's Evandale facilities, located near Cincinnati, Ohio, continued work on the Direct Cycle Propulsion System, which was designated XMA-1.

116
00:10:33,559 --> 00:10:42,500
This model illustrates how the XMA-1 system consisted of two X-211 turbojet engines with a reactor fitted between them.

117
00:10:43,719 --> 00:10:47,100
Reactor control rods were located in the forward section of the reactor.

118
00:10:47,100 --> 00:10:53,580
This cross-section view shows how compressed air would enter and leave the reactor through a series

119
00:10:53,580 --> 00:11:00,540
of wavy ducts. This design allowed the passage of air but trapped the straight line nuclear

120
00:11:00,540 --> 00:11:07,460
radiation. A full-scale mock-up of the XMA1 propulsion system was also constructed with

121
00:11:07,460 --> 00:11:14,879
the simulated reactor heat source in the center. A comparison with a conventional J79 chemical

122
00:11:14,879 --> 00:11:25,399
engine give some idea of the X211 engine in size and power. For instance, the J79 weighs 3,400

123
00:11:25,399 --> 00:11:37,439
pounds. The new X211, 15,700 pounds. J79 has 17 stages of compressor stators, six of these stages

124
00:11:37,440 --> 00:11:46,380
being variable. The X-211 has 16 stages, all variable. J-79 sea-level thrust with afterburner,

125
00:11:46,780 --> 00:11:57,640
15,600 pounds. The X-211, 34,400 pounds. It should also be noted that while it will operate on

126
00:11:57,640 --> 00:12:04,320
chemical fuel, the X-211 was designed and built and is at its most efficient under nuclear power.

127
00:12:05,320 --> 00:12:09,460
Four of these engines had been successfully tested with chemical fuel only

128
00:12:09,460 --> 00:12:14,660
in a large test cell which had covered air intakes and tall exhaust stacks.

129
00:12:15,540 --> 00:12:20,360
This test setup closely simulated the XMA-1 reactor engine combination

130
00:12:20,360 --> 00:12:24,280
with an X-211 engine on the left and a dummy engine on the right.

131
00:12:25,860 --> 00:12:29,060
The reactor was simulated by a center heat chamber

132
00:12:29,060 --> 00:12:33,900
which produced temperatures, pressures, and fluctuations simulating those of the reactor.

133
00:12:34,320 --> 00:12:42,080
This engine, the third, was operated for 30 hours under chemical fuel at low 1100 degrees

134
00:12:42,080 --> 00:12:43,600
turbine temperatures.

135
00:12:43,600 --> 00:12:48,320
Eventually, it will be run at 1700 degrees.

136
00:12:48,320 --> 00:12:52,580
The first twin engine running was scheduled for the third quarter of 1959.

137
00:12:52,580 --> 00:12:58,980
Concurrently, work on flight reactor development was also being conducted in General Electric's

138
00:12:58,980 --> 00:13:02,440
Evendale Aircraft Nuclear Propulsion Department.

139
00:13:02,440 --> 00:13:09,100
Here, nuclear power generation was being simulated in fuel element testing by the use of induction

140
00:13:09,100 --> 00:13:10,800
heating.

141
00:13:10,800 --> 00:13:15,340
An intensive research program was also being conducted to develop and evaluate high-temperature

142
00:13:15,340 --> 00:13:18,520
metallic fuel element materials.

143
00:13:18,520 --> 00:13:24,280
New chromium-based alloys of improved strength and workability were being developed.

144
00:13:24,280 --> 00:13:28,980
In a similar way, moderator materials were being developed to improve hydrogen retention

145
00:13:28,980 --> 00:13:33,940
and metallurgical stability at elevated temperatures.

146
00:13:33,940 --> 00:13:36,940
Other materials were also being examined for possible use

147
00:13:36,940 --> 00:13:39,139
in the reactor core.

148
00:13:39,139 --> 00:13:43,480
For example, hydrolysis tests were being conducted on beryllium oxide fuel elements

149
00:13:43,480 --> 00:13:47,159
under maximum temperature conditions.

150
00:13:47,159 --> 00:13:50,159
In 1958, however, a chromium fuel element

151
00:13:50,159 --> 00:13:53,440
with a clad hydrated zirconium yttrium alloy moderator

152
00:13:53,440 --> 00:13:56,740
looked promising for the tactical reactor core.

153
00:13:56,740 --> 00:14:02,200
Schedules called for the first direct cycle nuclear propulsion system ground test in the spring of 1961.

154
00:14:03,500 --> 00:14:07,340
The entire General Electric program cannot, of course, be covered here,

155
00:14:07,659 --> 00:14:10,440
but those were the major propulsion program highlights.

156
00:14:11,680 --> 00:14:18,500
The other propulsion system under investigation is Pratt & Whitney's liquid metal cooled indirect cycle system.

157
00:14:19,539 --> 00:14:24,200
As I mentioned earlier, at the termination of the Weapon System 125A program,

158
00:14:24,200 --> 00:14:30,120
the decision was made to concentrate on General Electric's air-cooled direct cycle system for early flight.

159
00:14:31,180 --> 00:14:36,920
As a result, the Pratt & Whitney indirect cycle system was reoriented toward long-range objectives,

160
00:14:37,280 --> 00:14:39,840
causing severe cutbacks in early development effort.

161
00:14:41,300 --> 00:14:45,240
For example, the work on the circulating fuel reactor propulsion system,

162
00:14:45,560 --> 00:14:50,720
which was a joint Oak Ridge-Pratt & Whitney program, was first cut back, then canceled.

163
00:14:50,720 --> 00:14:56,940
This included the aircraft reactor test at Oak Ridge and all conversion work on

164
00:14:56,940 --> 00:15:03,019
the Pratt & Whitney J91 chemical engine. At the present time, Pratt & Whitney is

165
00:15:03,019 --> 00:15:07,340
concentrating on a high-performance solid-fuel liquid metal cooled reactor

166
00:15:07,340 --> 00:15:13,500
with all nuclear supersonic flight potential. This system also operates by

167
00:15:13,500 --> 00:15:19,279
using a solid fuel reactor to supply heat to various type power plants, but in

168
00:15:19,279 --> 00:15:24,339
In this case the reactor is liquid metal cooled with a coolant circulating through radiators

169
00:15:24,339 --> 00:15:31,019
placed inside the engines where it transfers its heat to the air leaving the engine compressors.

170
00:15:31,019 --> 00:15:36,240
The liquid metal coolant under consideration is lithium 7.

171
00:15:36,240 --> 00:15:41,439
If as in early models this coolant is sodium, a secondary loop is needed with an intermediate

172
00:15:41,439 --> 00:15:43,360
heat exchanger.

173
00:15:43,360 --> 00:15:48,100
This is because sodium carries appreciable amounts of radiation from the reactor core

174
00:15:48,100 --> 00:15:50,680
while lithium does not.

175
00:15:50,680 --> 00:15:53,779
For this reason, using lithium, the secondary circuit

176
00:15:53,779 --> 00:15:57,100
would not be necessary if an oxidation-resistant radiator

177
00:15:57,100 --> 00:16:00,700
compatible with lithium can be developed.

178
00:16:00,700 --> 00:16:05,879
The indirect cycle work is being accomplished at the Air Force's recently completed Connecticut Aircraft

179
00:16:05,879 --> 00:16:10,440
Nuclear Engine Laboratory near Middletown, Connecticut.

180
00:16:10,440 --> 00:16:17,320
The first phase of the reoriented program consists of reactor research and component development.

181
00:16:17,320 --> 00:16:22,420
Primary emphasis at the present time centers around the development of a suitable liquid metal coolant

182
00:16:22,420 --> 00:16:24,800
and the necessary containment materials.

183
00:16:25,920 --> 00:16:31,000
As part of this study, molten lithium has been circulated through forced convection loops.

184
00:16:31,660 --> 00:16:37,200
The loop tubing is made of various materials to test corrosion and mass transport effects.

185
00:16:38,540 --> 00:16:41,920
In this instance, the containing material is a columbium alloy

186
00:16:41,920 --> 00:16:46,200
clad on the airside with stainless steel to protect the alloy from oxidation.

187
00:16:48,040 --> 00:16:52,840
Other forced convection loops are operated inside huge pressure tanks, in which the air has

188
00:16:52,840 --> 00:16:58,660
been replaced with an inert gas to prevent oxidation by outside air during the tests.

189
00:16:58,660 --> 00:17:05,019
Temperatures of 2,000 degrees Fahrenheit have been sustained for 250 hours.

190
00:17:05,019 --> 00:17:10,380
More than 25,000 hours of these studies have shown that columbium alloys give the most promise

191
00:17:10,380 --> 00:17:18,319
for reactor structures because of their high temperature strength and resistance to mass transport with lithium. Of prime

192
00:17:18,319 --> 00:17:21,859
importance at Pratt & Whitney is the construction of a forced convection loop

193
00:17:21,859 --> 00:17:27,559
for testing fuel element assemblies in nuclear test reactors. The reactor test

194
00:17:27,559 --> 00:17:31,819
loop resembles a miniature liquid-cooled reactor except that it contains a

195
00:17:31,819 --> 00:17:37,279
subcritical amount of fuel. In preparation for testing in Idaho's

196
00:17:37,279 --> 00:17:41,539
engineering test reactor, a full-scale loop was operated under non-nuclear

197
00:17:41,539 --> 00:17:47,279
conditions. A 1,000 kilowatt oil furnace was constructed to supply the heat which

198
00:17:47,279 --> 00:17:52,819
in the test reactor will be provided by a 5-inch fuel element. It was necessary

199
00:17:52,819 --> 00:17:56,299
to place the loop high up above the furnace to provide the proper heat and

200
00:17:56,299 --> 00:18:01,639
circulation environment. The reactor test loop was scheduled for operation in the

201
00:18:01,639 --> 00:18:08,220
engineering test reactor at Idaho during 1959. Other components of the indirect

202
00:18:08,220 --> 00:18:14,019
cycle system were also undergoing intensive testing. In this instance, data are being

203
00:18:14,019 --> 00:18:21,420
gathered on a small-scale model of the heat radiator. Bolton sodium potassium alloy, NAK,

204
00:18:21,880 --> 00:18:27,360
is passed through the radiator, making it red hot, as it will be in the engine. And,

205
00:18:27,420 --> 00:18:32,160
as will happen in flight, a blast of compressed air is loosed on the radiator, subjecting

206
00:18:32,160 --> 00:18:37,240
it to extreme thermal shock. This same effect will occur in flight when the pilot buries

207
00:18:37,240 --> 00:18:40,460
the air flow with the controls.

208
00:18:40,460 --> 00:18:45,299
Quarter section radiators were scheduled to be tested under various simulated flight conditions

209
00:18:45,299 --> 00:18:50,620
in the newly constructed four million dollar pilot radiator laboratory.

210
00:18:50,620 --> 00:18:56,099
Other canal facilities include a pump turbine laboratory for testing full scale liquid metal

211
00:18:56,099 --> 00:19:02,680
pumps and pump components, a heat exchange laboratory for testing liquid metal to liquid

212
00:19:02,680 --> 00:19:08,779
metal heat exchangers, an air laboratory for testing radiator turning vanes and other air

213
00:19:08,779 --> 00:19:15,120
components, a special hazards facility for determining liquid metal air and liquid metal

214
00:19:15,120 --> 00:19:22,600
water reactions, a nuclear physics laboratory for conducting reactor critical experiments,

215
00:19:22,600 --> 00:19:27,700
and a general laboratory for chemical and metallurgical research.

216
00:19:27,700 --> 00:19:33,420
The present indirect cycle research and development phase will continue through mid-1959.

217
00:19:33,420 --> 00:19:37,539
By that time it is estimated that the program will have progressed to the point where the

218
00:19:37,539 --> 00:19:44,819
fabrication of an experimental aircraft reactor will be considered by the AEC.

219
00:19:44,819 --> 00:19:49,680
As you can see, this is a vast propulsion system program, with both General Electric

220
00:19:49,680 --> 00:19:54,319
and Pratt & Whitney conducting extensive investigations.

221
00:19:54,319 --> 00:19:59,939
As of the last of 1958, we expected to have a direct cycle nuclear system in ground operation

222
00:19:59,939 --> 00:20:00,939
by mid-1961.

223
00:20:00,939 --> 00:20:06,220
Now this isn't the entire picture, of course.

224
00:20:06,220 --> 00:20:11,839
Closely connected to the propulsion program is the question of shielding.

225
00:20:11,839 --> 00:20:17,279
As you may recall, shielding for the nuclear aircraft has been divided with a shadow shield

226
00:20:17,279 --> 00:20:22,759
around the reactor and additional shielding around the crew compartment.

227
00:20:22,759 --> 00:20:27,779
This arrangement subjects all parts of the plane to radiation but allows a slimmer, more

228
00:20:27,779 --> 00:20:32,440
aerodynamic configuration with an earlier flight potential.

229
00:20:32,440 --> 00:20:38,819
Actually shielding investigation can be broken into two sections, experimental and theoretical.

230
00:20:38,819 --> 00:20:43,079
One of the greatest contributors to experimental work has been Convair, a division of General

231
00:20:43,079 --> 00:20:46,579
Dynamics at their Fort Worth plant.

232
00:20:46,579 --> 00:20:52,699
As you recall, late in 1955, and working with Oak Ridge National Laboratory, they converted

233
00:20:52,699 --> 00:20:59,399
a B-36 into a nuclear test airplane to investigate the split shield concept.

234
00:20:59,399 --> 00:21:02,039
This was not a nuclear-powered airplane.

235
00:21:02,039 --> 00:21:08,819
Its engines were the conventional chemically-fueled engines of the B-36, but it did carry an operating

236
00:21:08,819 --> 00:21:14,079
water-cooled, 1-megawatt reactor in the Bombay area.

237
00:21:14,079 --> 00:21:18,319
Following the split-shield concept, the reactor was shielded with shielding

238
00:21:18,319 --> 00:21:21,439
around the crew compartment also.

239
00:21:21,439 --> 00:21:26,079
A series of in-flight radiation tests demonstrated the feasibility of this concept

240
00:21:26,079 --> 00:21:30,519
and produced information regarding the detrimental effect of decreasing air density

241
00:21:30,519 --> 00:21:32,460
at higher altitudes.

242
00:21:32,460 --> 00:21:37,240
The flights also provided the first substantial data regarding the effects of radiation

243
00:21:37,240 --> 00:21:40,299
on aircraft structures.

244
00:21:40,299 --> 00:21:47,700
Following the flight tests, the reactor shield and the crew shield were transported to Oak Ridge, where they were positioned in the tower facility.

245
00:21:49,140 --> 00:21:53,140
The reactor was then operated, continuing the study of shielding effectiveness.

246
00:21:54,180 --> 00:21:58,740
By 1958, many new shielding materials were being investigated,

247
00:21:58,740 --> 00:22:05,300
including reactor shielding materials such as lithium hydride, beryllium oxide, tungsten alloys,

248
00:22:05,300 --> 00:22:11,620
and depleted uranium. While Oak Ridge continued to be the leader in theoretical studies and

249
00:22:11,620 --> 00:22:16,880
experimental testing, material testing was also going on at other facilities.

250
00:22:16,880 --> 00:22:21,400
For example, the Battelle Memorial Institute swimming pool reactor was

251
00:22:21,400 --> 00:22:26,620
being used to conduct shielding tests. General Electric's shielding activity

252
00:22:26,620 --> 00:22:29,680
was soon to be stepped up with the completion of the previously mentioned

253
00:22:29,680 --> 00:22:34,820
shield test pool facilities. Theoretically speaking, shielding

254
00:22:34,820 --> 00:22:39,500
computations based on these experiments have produced encouraging results.

255
00:22:39,500 --> 00:22:46,519
For example, given a weight, it was now possible to predict the resultant dosage to the crew within allowable limits.

256
00:22:46,519 --> 00:22:51,720
This program may be summed up by saying that shielding materials were pretty well set,

257
00:22:51,720 --> 00:22:55,680
although fabrication procedures were something of a problem.

258
00:22:55,680 --> 00:23:00,380
We could, however, fly a first development aircraft with existing shielding designs,

259
00:23:00,380 --> 00:23:05,160
but more in-flight data would be needed for an operational aircraft.

260
00:23:05,160 --> 00:23:10,120
Much the same can be said for the radiation effects program.

261
00:23:10,120 --> 00:23:15,700
Radiation concern has been confined to fast neutrons and gamma rays, and their deteriorating

262
00:23:15,700 --> 00:23:21,800
effect on organic materials, as well as their effect on metals under stress.

263
00:23:21,800 --> 00:23:27,279
The effect of induced radioactivity for prolonged periods after exposure and radiation effects

264
00:23:27,279 --> 00:23:33,519
on electrical components and electrical fields were also being investigated.

265
00:23:33,519 --> 00:23:38,400
To supply this critical information, a series of dynamic tests have been conducted at Convair's

266
00:23:38,400 --> 00:23:42,440
ground test reactor facility near Fort Worth.

267
00:23:42,440 --> 00:23:46,900
Complete subsystems were mounted on platforms and instrumented.

268
00:23:46,900 --> 00:23:51,039
The systems were then remotely moved into place near the reactor, where they were operated

269
00:23:51,039 --> 00:23:54,240
within the high flux field.

270
00:23:54,240 --> 00:23:59,440
It is significant to note that during these tests, failure due to nuclear radiation occurred

271
00:23:59,440 --> 00:24:03,340
in 46% of all systems.

272
00:24:03,340 --> 00:24:08,920
Lockheed Aircraft's newly-constructed 10-megawatt radiation effects reactor provides the means

273
00:24:08,920 --> 00:24:13,720
of dynamically testing complete systems in a high-flux field.

274
00:24:13,720 --> 00:24:19,480
After the systems are mounted on a flat car, they are moved into position near the reactor.

275
00:24:19,480 --> 00:24:22,680
The critical reactor is then raised to the proper height.

276
00:24:22,680 --> 00:24:27,940
Thus, the complete system can be operated while under irradiation.

277
00:24:27,940 --> 00:24:32,840
As many as six systems may be operated here simultaneously.

278
00:24:32,840 --> 00:24:37,759
In addition, the newly constructed Air Force Nuclear Engineering test facility at Wright

279
00:24:37,759 --> 00:24:42,360
Air Development Center may soon provide the information necessary to develop radiation

280
00:24:42,360 --> 00:24:45,080
resistant materials.

281
00:24:45,080 --> 00:24:49,980
The reduced radiation effects program was of necessity a limited effort.

282
00:24:49,980 --> 00:24:55,059
However, it was the belief of personnel working in this area and in shielding that while the

283
00:24:55,059 --> 00:24:59,599
If the present state of the art would not support a full scale strategic weapon system,

284
00:24:59,599 --> 00:25:04,960
it would support a developmental prototype nuclear powered aircraft.

285
00:25:04,960 --> 00:25:09,419
Research efforts in the life sciences were directly linked with progress in other fields

286
00:25:09,419 --> 00:25:15,079
such as shielding, irradiation and the planned use of strategic aircraft.

287
00:25:15,079 --> 00:25:19,419
As a result of the program to date, enough data had been obtained to ensure the safety

288
00:25:19,419 --> 00:25:22,339
of the crew with present shielding.

289
00:25:22,339 --> 00:25:26,859
At this time, it was considered possible for a crew to fly for relatively prolonged periods

290
00:25:26,859 --> 00:25:29,980
without dangerous effects.

291
00:25:29,980 --> 00:25:35,659
The safety analysis program was also being conducted on a somewhat limited scale.

292
00:25:35,659 --> 00:25:40,319
Part of this study included radiation release tests conducted in Idaho.

293
00:25:40,319 --> 00:25:46,220
Here, radioactive material was deliberately released, along with smoke for plotting purposes,

294
00:25:46,220 --> 00:25:51,799
to investigate the effects of a nuclear aircraft crash and reactor burnup.

295
00:25:51,799 --> 00:25:56,000
of these and weather effects studies indicated that nuclear considerations

296
00:25:56,000 --> 00:26:01,039
create no undue hazard to civilian populations if adequate operational

297
00:26:01,039 --> 00:26:08,180
controls are exercised. Of extreme significance to the program was the 1958

298
00:26:08,180 --> 00:26:14,079
presidential decision to bypass a purely testbed or demonstration aircraft and to

299
00:26:14,079 --> 00:26:18,500
concentrate on the development of a useful military weapon. At the close of

300
00:26:18,500 --> 00:26:24,819
1958, two companies, Lockheed and Convair, were in competition for this development contract.

301
00:26:25,480 --> 00:26:28,920
Both had long been associated with the ANP project.

302
00:26:30,880 --> 00:26:36,160
Lockheed Aircrafts Program was begun in the spring of 1951 with a nuclear propulsion study.

303
00:26:36,859 --> 00:26:42,720
In 1955, as part of the Weapon System 125A program, they were asked to design an airframe

304
00:26:42,720 --> 00:26:45,460
for Pratt & Whitney's closed-cycle propulsion system.

305
00:26:45,460 --> 00:26:54,460
During this design period, Lockheed also conducted studies of a combination tug-toe strategic system called Blackjack.

306
00:26:54,460 --> 00:27:01,460
After the launching of Russia's Sputnik, several quick-fly or testbed designs were investigated,

307
00:27:01,460 --> 00:27:05,460
but these were canceled by the development aircraft decision.

308
00:27:05,460 --> 00:27:14,460
By the end of 1958, Lockheed had submitted their proposal for a developmental aircraft based on applicability to the current Camel concept.

309
00:27:14,460 --> 00:27:19,740
In addition, Lockheed was conducting limited studies of a manned aircraft employing both

310
00:27:19,740 --> 00:27:24,519
Pratt & Whitney turbojet and ramjet nuclear propulsion systems.

311
00:27:24,519 --> 00:27:29,680
These studies presented designs of advanced aircraft having high altitude supersonic flight

312
00:27:29,680 --> 00:27:32,680
capabilities.

313
00:27:32,680 --> 00:27:36,660
Convair has experienced a somewhat similar development, since they also began a study

314
00:27:36,660 --> 00:27:39,220
program early in 1951.

315
00:27:39,220 --> 00:27:46,400
Similarly, in 1955, also on the Weapon System 125A Program, they were teamed with General

316
00:27:46,400 --> 00:27:49,819
Electric and the Direct Cycle Propulsion System.

317
00:27:49,819 --> 00:27:56,000
Following original program requirements, they first developed their Model 25 concept.

318
00:27:56,000 --> 00:28:02,100
Then came a design called Tarpon, followed by the GE Propulsion System Testbed, which

319
00:28:02,100 --> 00:28:08,860
was a version of Tarpon and the Model 54 design for the Camel Development Airplane Competition,

320
00:28:08,860 --> 00:28:11,100
which was completed by the end of 1958.

321
00:28:12,600 --> 00:28:16,780
Early in 1959, and in time to be included here,

322
00:28:16,780 --> 00:28:21,180
the decision was made as a result of the previously mentioned competition

323
00:28:21,180 --> 00:28:25,240
to award the Development Airplane Design Contract to Convair

324
00:28:25,240 --> 00:28:27,400
when the decision was made to go ahead.

325
00:28:28,860 --> 00:28:33,620
This aircraft would be powered by the GE Direct Cycle propulsion system.

326
00:28:33,620 --> 00:28:35,800
Design work on this aircraft began.

327
00:28:38,860 --> 00:28:48,340
As you can see, this is a huge program and it's impossible for us to cover every area of development.

328
00:28:49,880 --> 00:28:56,140
However, the GE direct cycle propulsion system was currently undergoing dynamic component testing.

329
00:28:57,220 --> 00:29:01,640
The Pratt & Whitney indirect cycle system was in an intensive developmental stage

330
00:29:01,640 --> 00:29:06,700
and an experimental reactor was forecast to be ready for operation in 1963.

331
00:29:08,860 --> 00:29:14,780
The various support programs, shielding radiation effects, radiobiology and safety,

332
00:29:14,780 --> 00:29:21,780
were keeping pace with the planned major system delivery schedules with the objective of a nuclear flight in 1964.

333
00:29:23,900 --> 00:29:32,700
ANP progress in the development of nuclear propulsion and power systems for unmanned weapon systems will be covered in a separate film.

334
00:29:32,700 --> 00:29:36,799
at the successful conclusion of these programs

335
00:29:36,799 --> 00:29:40,980
we will possess a means of delivering huge payloads over unlimited ranges

336
00:29:40,980 --> 00:29:48,340
with a minimum of foreign commitments that and a knowledge of nuclear power application second to none

337
00:29:48,340 --> 00:29:50,140
thank you gentlemen
